                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ




Beyond the Armory wall I could feel the borough’s rooms.
   I couldn’t read anyone’s thoughts, only how the air sat in each room.
Narrow stairwells, high ceilings, windows cracked over traffic, tired
speakers all carrying the same buried interval. Don Chino’s bodega. A
barbershop with one chair empty. The Ballroom’s ceiling speaker. A
gift wrapped in a dish towel atop a refrigerator in 4C. A nap-time ma‐
chine in a daycare closet. A bus asleep in a depot, a tone still humming
in its ceiling. A million kitchens, some of them loud, because Pri’s mes‐
sage had gone out at 9:39 and people had turned their own music up.
The pressed place went hunting for its old home and found the floor
sliding away.
   Somewhere in the hall a backup speaker was still playing the recep‐
tion song, and it stayed a song. A phone near it played rain, and the
rain stayed rain.
   The weight beneath them loosened.
   Across the borough it came back to people the way feeling comes
back into a foot that fell asleep. I only know the stories we collected
later. A man on the Grand Concourse who started crying at a mattress
commercial and couldn’t say why. Two sisters in Soundview who
picked up an argument they’d dropped in January, exactly where
they’d left it. A whole Bx1, past midnight, that started talking at once.
Desmond heard about that bus for a year.
   I wanted to hear every room let go. I wanted proof, a ledger of
everything we had done, and that hunger was wearing the costume of
conscience. My attention stretched out over the borough room by
room, and the minutes quit counting themselves.
   Yanaisa smacked the back of my hand and I felt nothing.
   Mikey planted himself in front of me.
   His lips were moving. Only the near edge of his voice reached me, as
though he were talking from behind a door.
   “Let go.”
   I tried.




